# Telemetry

Howdy traces your app with [OpenTelemetry](https://opentelemetry.io/): every
request, database query, remote call, email and sign-in becomes a span, and
spans that belong together form a trace you can follow from the request
down to the SQL it ran.

Nothing is recorded or sent until you ask for it. Without `howdy_telemetry`
the spans cost next to nothing and go nowhere; with it, you choose where
they go:

- to an OpenTelemetry collector or any backend that takes OTLP, such as
  Grafana Tempo, Honeycomb or Jaeger, in production
- into memory, for the [admin area](/docs/admin/telemetry) to show while
  you develop

## Install

```toml
[dependencies]
howdy_telemetry = { git = "https://github.com/mikeyjones/howdy.git", ref = "v1.0.0", path = "telemetry" }
```

## Sending traces to a collector

Start telemetry once, early in `main`:

```gleam
import howdy/telemetry

pub fn main() {
  let assert Ok(Nil) =
    telemetry.new("acme-web")
    |> telemetry.otlp
    |> telemetry.start
  // open the database, start the app...
}
```

`otlp` sends spans over OTLP/HTTP in batches, configured by the standard
environment variables, so endpoints and keys stay out of the code:

- `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`: where to send, such as
  `https://api.honeycomb.io`. Defaults to `http://localhost:4318`.
- `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS`: headers for every export, such as
  `x-honeycomb-team=KEY`.
- `OTEL_SERVICE_NAME`: overrides the service name given to `new`.
- `OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES`: more resource attributes, such as
  `deployment.environment.name=staging`.
- `OTEL_SDK_DISABLED`: `true` turns everything off again, even after
  `start`.

To send only where a collector is configured, and stay silent everywhere
else, use `from_env`. It gives a configuration only when
`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` or `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` is set:

```gleam
case telemetry.from_env("acme-web") {
  Ok(config) -> {
    let assert Ok(Nil) = telemetry.start(config)
    Nil
  }
  Error(Nil) -> Nil
}
```

`otlp_to(endpoint:, headers:)` names the collector in code instead.
`resource("service.version", "1.4.2")` describes the service further. A
short-lived program, such as a task run from the command line, should call
`telemetry.flush()` before it exits, so the last batch is not lost.

### Trying it locally

Jaeger's all-in-one image takes OTLP on port 4318 and has a UI on 16686:

```sh
docker run --rm -p 4318:4318 -p 16686:16686 jaegertracing/jaeger:2.10.0
```

Grafana's `grafana/otel-lgtm` image does the same with Tempo, Loki and
Grafana.

## Sampling

Every trace is recorded by default. On a busy site, keep a share of them:

```gleam
telemetry.new("acme-web")
|> telemetry.otlp
|> telemetry.sample(0.1)
```

A request that arrives from another traced service keeps that service's
decision, so a trace is recorded throughout or not at all. The
`OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER` variables override this.

## Recording in development

In development, record into memory and give the recorder to the admin:

```gleam
// dev/my_app_dev.gleam
import howdy/telemetry
import howdy/telemetry/recorder

let recorder = recorder.new(keep: 200)
let assert Ok(Nil) =
  telemetry.new("acme-web")
  |> telemetry.record(recorder)
  |> telemetry.start
let dashboard = admin.new() |> admin.telemetry(recorder)
```

`record` and `otlp` can be combined, to see traces in the admin and a
collector at once. See [the admin's telemetry pages](/docs/admin/telemetry).

## What gets traced

See [tracing](/docs/telemetry/tracing) for the spans Howdy opens and how to
add your own, and [logs](/docs/telemetry/logs) for how log lines join the
trace they were written in.
